---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import DocsFigure from '../../components/DocsFigure.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/catalog';
import { metrics } from '../../lib/metrics';

// Listed from the measures themselves, so this page names them as the charts do.
const totals = metrics.filter((metric) => metric.additive);
const perBrand = metrics.filter((metric) => !metric.additive);
---

<Docs
  title="Reading the charts"
  lede="The Chart page draws every brand, grouped and sized by a figure you choose. This guide covers what the circles and boxes mean and how to change them."
>
  <h2 id="boxes">Boxes within boxes</h2>
  <p>
    The chart is a <em>treemap</em>: each brand is a box, and the brands are grouped into larger boxes, such as a country or an
    ownership group. A box's area stands for the figure the chart is sized by, so a bigger box means more of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href={href('/chart/')}>Chart</a> page opens with every brand as a circle inside the group that owns it. Each brand shows its
    logo where there's room, and each group its logo across the top of its circle.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="chart-overview.png" alt="Ownership groups as circles, each holding a circle per brand with the brand's logo, and the group's logo across its top." wide>
    The chart as it opens: brands inside their owners.
  </DocsFigure>
  <p>
    Drawn as rectangles and grouped by nation of origin, there is one box per country, sized by how many brands come from there. The
    number by each name counts its brands, and the box shows as many of their logos as fit, then how many more there are.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="brands-overview.png" alt="The Brands chart: a box per country, largest first, each showing a count and a grid of brand logos." wide>
    The Brands chart, grouped by nation of origin. Switzerland has the most brands, so its box is largest.
  </DocsFigure>

  <h2 id="size-by">Choosing what sizes the chart</h2>
  <p>The <strong>Size by</strong> menu above the chart picks the figure. Its choices come in two groups.</p>
  <DocsFigure src="controls.png" alt="The chart's controls: a Size by menu and a Draw as menu." width={540} />
  <h3>Totals for each box</h3>
  <p>These figures add up, so a group's box is the sum of its brands and boxes can be compared across the whole chart:</p>
  <ul>{totals.map((metric) => <li>{metric.label}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>
    As rectangles sized by number of brands, each box is drawn whole; as circles, each brand is a circle of the same size inside its
    group. Sized by anything else in this group, each box opens up to show its brands as boxes of their own.
  </p>
  <h3>Within one box</h3>
  <p>
    These figures don't add up. A country's brands don't have a combined age, for example. So they are offered once you zoom into a
    single box:
  </p>
  <ul>{perBrand.map((metric) => <li>{metric.label}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>
    Brands with no figure yet for the chosen measure are left out of the sizing. The line under the chart says how many, and inside a
    zoomed box they are listed by name.
  </p>

  <h2 id="zoom">Zooming into a box</h2>
  <p>
    Click a box or circle, or its name, to see its brands alone. From there, <strong>All by …</strong> returns to the whole chart, and so does the
    Escape key. Where the box has a page of its own, such as a country or a group, a link to it appears beside its name.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="zoom.mp4" alt="A recording: the pointer clicks Switzerland, the chart zooms into its brands, hovers two brands to show their details, and returns." wide>
    Zooming into Switzerland, where brands are sized by age. Hovering over a brand shows its figures.
  </DocsFigure>
  <DocsFigure src="brands-zoomed.png" alt="Switzerland zoomed in: brands as boxes of different sizes, larger ones showing logos and smaller ones names." wide>
    Inside a box, the largest brands show their logos and smaller ones their names. Narrow boxes set the name sideways.
  </DocsFigure>

  <h2 id="group-by">Grouping</h2>
  <p><strong>Group by</strong> sorts the brands into boxes by:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Owning group</strong>: the corporation that owns each brand today. A brand with several part-owners sits in each of their boxes, but is counted once.</li>
    <li><strong>Nation of origin</strong>: where the brand was founded, which may differ from where it is run or made today.</li>
    <li><strong>Segment</strong>: where the brand sits in the market, from haute horlogerie to budget.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing</strong>: every brand in one box.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Brands with nothing recorded for the grouping aren't drawn as a box of their own, since a gap in the data would otherwise be the
    biggest box. They are listed under the chart instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="revenue">Revenue and other business</h2>
  <p>
    Grouped by owner and sized by revenue, a group can record
    its total revenue as well as its brands'. Whatever its watch brands don't account for, such as jewelry, fashion or leather goods,
    shows as a hatched block. <strong>Include groups' other business</strong> turns those blocks on and off.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="groups-revenue.png" alt="Ownership groups sized by annual revenue: Richemont's box is mostly a hatched other-business block beside Cartier and Vacheron Constantin." wide>
    Sized by annual revenue. Most of Richemont's revenue comes from outside its watch brands.
  </DocsFigure>
  <p>
    A brand with several part-owners sits inside each of them and, by default, counts its whole revenue toward every one.
    Where a drawn brand is part-owned, <strong>Count part-owned brands by stake</strong> credits each owner with only its share,
    such as 20% of the brand's revenue for a 20% stake. A stake that isn't public is still counted in full.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sized by watches made a year, a brand made under license counts toward the group that makes it rather than the one that owns the
    name, and its box has a dashed edge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="circles">Rectangles or circles</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Draw as</strong> switches the same chart between rectangles and circles packed inside one another. Circles show each box's
    size less precisely, but make the nesting easy to see. Circles show logos by the same rules as rectangles: a brand's whole logo where
    its name is large enough to read, otherwise its name or symbol alone, and its name in text where the circle is too small for a logo.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="size-and-shape.mp4" alt="A recording: Size by changes to annual revenue, then Draw as changes to circles, then Size by returns to number of brands." wide>
    Sizing ownership groups by revenue, then drawing them as circles.
  </DocsFigure>
  <div class="pair">
    <DocsFigure src="groups-circles.png" alt="Ownership groups as circles, sized by number of brands, each holding a same-sized circle per brand." wide>
      By number of brands.
    </DocsFigure>
    <DocsFigure src="groups-revenue-circles.png" alt="Ownership groups as circles sized by revenue, with brand circles inside each group's circle." wide>
      By annual revenue, with each group's brands inside it.
    </DocsFigure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="filters">Following the filters</h2>
  <p>
    The chart follows the filters above it: it draws only the brands that match, and the line under it says so.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="follow-filters.mp4" alt="A recording: the owner filter is set to Richemont and the chart narrows to its brands, then to Swatch Group." wide>
    Filtering by owner narrows the chart to that group's brands.
  </DocsFigure>

  <h2 id="notes">The notes under the chart</h2>
  <p>
    A line under the chart says what is drawn and what is left out. Brands left out for a missing figure, or with nothing recorded for
    the grouping, are listed below it as links.
  </p>
  <DocsFigure src="brands-note.png" alt="The note under a zoomed chart, counting the brands drawn and those with no figure yet." wide />
  <DocsFigure src="brands-unsized.png" alt="An opened list of brands with no figure for the chosen measure, each a link to its page." wide />

  <h2 id="remembered">Your choices are remembered</h2>
  <p>
    The chart remembers how you last set it, in this browser only. On a narrow screen the chart keeps a readable size and scrolls
    sideways.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: 0 16px;
  }
</style>
